<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Hisham I of Córdoba</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/757">757</a>–<a href="/years/796">796</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/8th-century">8th century</a> · Also known as Hisham I</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Hisham I was the second Umayyad emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>, ruling from 788 to 796, son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a>. A pious ruler who favoured the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of law, he sent annual summer campaigns against the Christian north, sacking <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a> in 794 and 795, and raided <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/septimania">Septimania</a> in 793. He completed his father's <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/hisham-i-of-cordoba.jpg" alt="Hisham I of Córdoba" width="320" height="160" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Numismática Pliego · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Dirham_hisham_i_21829.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Hisham was born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> on 26 April 757, the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/abd-al-rahman-i">Abd al-Rahman I</a> and his wife Halul and the younger half-brother of Suleiman. By reputation he was pious, sober and capable, and Abd al-Rahman favoured him. He came to the throne in 788 with considerable experience and the support of the religious elite.</p><p>He was at once opposed by his elder brother Sulayman, who ruled in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/toledo">Toledo</a>, was a seasoned fighter and enjoyed the loyalty of the Syrian troops. Sulayman recruited another brother, Abd Allah. After losing Toledo to Hisham and failing to regain the initiative, the two agreed to accept permanent exile in the Maghrib together with a large cash settlement.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His reign was comparatively stable. At home Hisham continued his father's work, completing the mosque of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a> and improving the city, including repairs to the great Roman bridge. He relied heavily on Umayyad family clients and entrenched the emerging <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maliki-school">Maliki school</a> of scholars in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/emirate">emirate</a>'s power structure; <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/malik-ibn-anas">Malik ibn Anas</a> himself was said to have praised him as an example of piety and justice.</p><p>In 793, facing Carolingian pressure across the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pyrenees">Pyrenees</a>, he called a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jihad">jihad</a> against the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> and sent troops to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/girona">Girona</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/narbonne">Narbonne</a>, which held out. His general Abd al-Malik ibn Abd al-Wahid ibn Mughith was more fortunate near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/carcassonne">Carcassonne</a>, where he defeated William of Orange. The expedition brought loot and many slaves, which paid for the expansion of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mosque-cathedral-of-cordoba">Great Mosque of Córdoba</a> and for new mosques.</p><p>From 794 Abd al-Malik and his brother Abd al-Karim campaigned each year against Álava, Old Castile and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/asturias">Asturias</a>, and in 794 sacked <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/oviedo">Oviedo</a>. Alfonso II of Asturias fled and opened contact with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charlemagne">Charlemagne</a>. These raids seem to have aimed at loot and at reasserting <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cordoba">Córdoba</a>'s military superiority over restive garrisons, lords and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/basques">Basques</a>, not at destroying the northern principalities.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Hisham died in 796 after a reign of eight years. He is described as living simply and avoiding ostentation, and was known for impartial justice and sound administration, earning the name the just. According to one account, he believed from his horoscope that he had only eight years to live and gave much of his wealth to charity. After his death Abd Allah returned from exile and claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/valencia">Valencia</a>, and Suleiman claimed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tangier">Tangiers</a>, against Hisham's son al-Hakam I.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Hisham I of Córdoba” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The emirs sent regular summer campaigns against the north: under <b>Hisham I</b> armies sacked Oviedo in 794 and 795 and raided Septimania in 793, though the Franks took Barcelona in 801.</blockquote><a href="/empire/emirate-of-cordoba">Emirate of Córdoba · Military</a></li><li><blockquote>Armies of the emir <b>Hisham I</b> sacked Oviedo in 794 and 795, but Alfonso II defeated one Muslim force at Lutos in 794, and in 798 he raided as far as Lisbon, sending captured booty to Charlemagne.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-asturias">Kingdom of Asturias · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Córdoba, in what is now <a href="/countries/spain">Spain</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=782" aria-label="Córdoba on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4431 801 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Hisham I of Córdoba"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4881 1071h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4881 1071h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=37.89,-4.78&amp;year=782">What was here in 782 CE? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
